---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = '/misc/big-bear-2025/';
---
<BaseLayout title="A Weekend Above the Clouds — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A short weekend trip to Big Bear Lake with our dog Wangcai: a cabin in the pines near Running Springs, the Rim of the World Highway, Big Bear Lake at golden hour and a Sunday morning at Lake Arrowhead.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July 2025</p>
  <h1>A Weekend Above the Clouds</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A short weekend trip to Big Bear Lake, with Wangcai and a window full of pines.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>3 days · July 18 – 20, 2025</strong><br />
    Running Springs → Rim of the World Highway → Big Bear Lake → Running Springs → Lake Arrowhead → home<br />
    the San Bernardino Mountains, all by car · two mountain lakes · one cabin in the pines ·
    one dog who was up before dawn
  </div>

  <p>
    Some trips are an expedition. This one was a weekend. In the middle of July, when the lowlands of Southern
    California bake, we drove up into the <strong>San Bernardino Mountains</strong> for two nights in a small cabin in
    the pines near <strong>Running Springs</strong>, a mountain town about six thousand feet up. From there, the plan was
    simple: one evening at <strong>Big Bear Lake</strong>, one morning at <strong>Lake Arrowhead</strong>, and as little
    else as possible in between.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was also a trip with <strong>Wangcai</strong>, our cream-colored dog, who had come home
    that January (her story is <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">here</a>). As with the other trips on this page, what
    follows is reconstructed from the camera roll. It is a short roll, a few dozen photos, and a surprising share of
    them are of her.
  </p>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-weekend.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-weekend.jpg'} alt="Map of the weekend in the San Bernardino Mountains: from Running Springs east along the Rim of the World Highway to Big Bear Lake and back, then west to Lake Arrowhead Village." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole weekend fits on one map: Running Springs in the middle, Big Bear Lake to the east, Lake Arrowhead to the west.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Friday night in the pines</h3>
  <p>
    We arrived on Friday evening. The first photo of the weekend was taken at 8:25 pm, as the light went: a portable
    gas grill and a green propane canister set out on a weathered wooden picnic table, the mat under them beaded with
    droplets, and the gravel beyond glowing orange under an outdoor lamp. In the end the grill went unused: dinner that night was hot pot. At this altitude the evening air cools
    quickly even in July, and the woods around the cabin go quiet and dark within minutes of sunset. It is not a
    glamorous picture, but it is exactly what the start of a mountain weekend looks like.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'cabin-grill.jpg'} alt="A black portable gas grill and a propane canister on a wooden picnic table at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Friday, 8:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Dawn, with a window full of pines</h3>
  <p>
    The best part of the cabin was its window, a single sheet of glass that turned one whole wall into a view of the
    forest. Wangcai found this out first. At 5:40 on Saturday morning, well before anyone else was ready to be awake,
    she was stretched out along the bottom of that window, paws up against the frame, staring into the trees. Outside, the
    tall trunks of the pines stood in the blue half-light before sunrise, with the forest floor between them still in
    shadow and only the sky showing any color.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-dawn-pines.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog lying with her paws up, looking out a large window at tall pine trunks in the blue light before sunrise." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>5:41 am. Someone is up.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Over the next ten minutes the camera followed her through the whole repertoire. First she sat up, very straight,
    ears high, and watched the forest like a sentry, her reflection floating faintly in the glass beside the trees.
    Whatever she was watching for, a squirrel or a jay or just the novelty of a world with no walls in it, she kept at it
    for a good while. Then, at 5:49, the watch ended the way most dog watches end: curled up in a tight crescent with
    her nose tucked in, asleep against the glass, the pines still standing guard outside.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-window.jpg'} alt="The dog sitting upright, gazing out a large window at pine trees, with her faint reflection in the glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On watch, 5:42 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-asleep.jpg'} alt="The dog curled up asleep beside the window, the forest visible through the glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Off watch, 5:49 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The Rim of the World</h3>
  <p>
    Late on Saturday afternoon we drove east toward Big Bear on the <strong>Rim of the World Highway</strong>. This is
    State Route 18, and the name is not much of an exaggeration: for long stretches the road runs along the crest of
    the San Bernardino Mountains, with the land dropping away on one side. It is one of the classic mountain drives of
    Southern California and a designated scenic byway, and from Running Springs eastward it climbs through dense
    conifer forest toward the high valley that holds Big Bear Lake.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rim-ridge.jpg'} alt="A forested mountain ridge with a pale band of rock across its flank, under a deep blue sky, with firs in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rim of the World Highway, just before 6 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rim-pines.jpg'} alt="A deep forested canyon framed by a pine branch, with white clouds over the far ridges." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The canyon below the road.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Just before six we pulled over to look. The view was the kind that makes you stop the car: a deep canyon blanketed
    in pine and fir, ridges stacked one behind another, and a pale band of rock running across the opposite slope. The
    sky was the saturated blue of high altitude, with a few fat white clouds drifting over. Looking east up the canyon,
    the trees parted just enough to show a small patch of blue water far in the distance, the first sign of the lakes
    that sit in these mountains.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rim-lake-glimpse.jpg'} alt="A wide view east over a forested canyon, with a small patch of lake visible in the distance below rolling hills." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A glimpse of water far up the canyon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rim-canyon.jpg'} alt="A closer view of the forested canyon slopes with pale rock outcrops among the trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Forest all the way down.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Big Bear Lake at golden hour</h3>
  <p>
    By half past six we were in <strong>Big Bear Lake</strong>. The first stop was <strong>Tea &amp; Coffee Exchange</strong>,
    a café on Paine Road, whose hand-drawn chalkboard listed a run of cat-themed mochas: a <em>Tabby</em> with white chocolate and caramel, a <em>Tuxedo</em> of white and
    dark chocolate, a <em>Tortoise Shell</em> (spelled "Tortise" on the board) and a <em>Calico</em>, illustrated with a
    ball of yarn and a cat stretched out mid-nap. Then, a little before seven, we walked down Paine Road to the water near the
    marina. Big Bear Lake is a reservoir, made when a dam was built across Bear Creek in 1884 to store water
    for the orange groves in the valley below, and today it is one of Southern California's favorite mountain escapes.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'big-bear-cat-mochas.jpg'} alt="A chalkboard menu of cat-themed mochas named Tabby, Tuxedo, Tortise Shell and Calico, decorated with cat faces and a ball of yarn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The menu at Tea &amp; Coffee Exchange, Big Bear Lake.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'big-bear-marina.jpg'} alt="A marina of moored boats on calm blue water at Big Bear Lake, with forested mountains beyond and willows in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Big Bear Lake, 6:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The lake that evening was calm and glassy, and the low sun turned it a deep blue. A long wooden dock ran out to
    rows of moored boats, and beyond them the far shore rose into dark, forested mountains under a cloudless sky.
    Willows crowded the edge of the water in the foreground. Lake Arrowhead, as we would see the next day, is tucked
    in among steep hills. Big Bear, by contrast, sits in a broad, open valley almost seven thousand feet up, and its
    shore feels wide, bright and unhurried.
  </p>

  <h3>Stars over Running Springs</h3>
  <p>
    Back at the cabin, at 10:40 that night, the camera pointed straight up. Away from city lights and more than a mile
    up, the sky over the mountains fills with far more stars than you ever see at sea level. A phone held up in the dark
    for a long exposure records them as a fine scatter of white points, framed at the edges by the black silhouettes of
    the pine tops, with one branch at the bottom of the frame lit gold by a lamp. These are not astrophotographs, but
    they are honest records of what it was like to stand outside and look up that night.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'night-sky.jpg'} alt="A dark blue night sky full of stars, with the silhouettes of trees at the lower edges." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>10:40 pm, looking up.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'night-sky-pines.jpg'} alt="A starry night sky above the dark silhouettes of pine tops, one branch lit gold from below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Stars through the pines.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Sunday morning at Lake Arrowhead</h3>
  <p>
    On Sunday we went the other way, west along the mountain to <strong>Lake Arrowhead</strong>, and by 11:25 we were on
    the waterfront in <strong>Lake Arrowhead Village</strong>. Like Big Bear, the lake is a reservoir. It began in the
    early twentieth century as Little Bear Lake and was renamed in the 1920s after the arrowhead-shaped landmark on the
    mountainside above San Bernardino. Unusually, the lake itself is private, and boating on it is reserved for
    members of the local lake association. From the village deck you look straight down on their docks: long rows of
    boat slips running out into deep-blue water, with forested hills and lakeside houses lining the far shore.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig"><img src={P + 'arrowhead-docks.jpg'} alt="Rows of boat slips on a floating dock stretching into a deep blue lake, with a forested shore beyond, seen from a deck railing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boat slips below the village deck, Lake Arrowhead.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Half an hour later, Wangcai led the way along the lakeside promenade. It was a cloudless midsummer
    noon, bright enough to make the water sparkle, and the village across the cove looked almost like a stage set:
    steep red roofs, half-timbered Tudor-style buildings and a slender church spire, with motorboats tied up along the
    shore. The look is deliberate. The old village was burned down in 1979, famously as a fire-department training
    exercise, and rebuilt in this storybook alpine style. It is a lovely place to walk a dog before the drive home,
    and that is what came next: from Lake Arrowhead we headed back down the mountain and drove home on Sunday.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig"><img src={P + 'arrowhead-village.jpg'} alt="A panorama of Lake Arrowhead Village across the water: half-timbered buildings with red roofs, a church spire and moored boats below forested hills." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Arrowhead Village from the promenade, just before noon.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That was the whole weekend: two nights, two lakes and one long look at the forest through a window. It was short
    by the standards of the other trips on this page, and Wangcai, by Saturday
    morning, had clearly decided that the best seat in the house was the one facing the trees.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Map built from the trip's photo timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been removed
    from the published images. Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors; each stop is placed where its photo was taken
    (Running Springs is marked at the town center), the cabin and home are never marked, and the dashed lines show
    the order of stops, not the exact roads.</small></p>
  <WangcaiSeries current="/misc/big-bear-2025" />
  <Comments />
</BaseLayout>

